// The compiler's own expression tree, and the one place in the language where a field has no name.
//
// `packages/wacc/src/ast.wac` is 468 lines and is the most matched-on type in the repository. It is
// also where a language asymmetry costs the most, and the asymmetry is not one vision invented:
//
//     Point q = Point { x: 3, y: 4 };     // a struct: by name, order-independent, nullables omitted
//     Shape b = Shape.Circle(2.0);        // a variant: by position, and only by position
//     case Circle(r): …                   // and read back by position too
//
// `spec/spec/structs.md` `[§wac-struct-named-4y8pg2j]` gives a struct's fields names on the way in
// and lets a nullable one be left out. An enum's payload fields **have** names — they are written
// `Binary(i32 op, Expr left, Expr right)` — and no code can use them, in either direction.
//
// ## What that costs here, counted
//
// Over `packages/` and the rest of the tree, excluding `spec/cases`:
//
//     812  match arms binding two or more payload fields
//     291  of those on a variant where two fields share a type
//      23  such variants, of which 13 are this file's
//
// A shared type is what makes the swap silent. `Ternary(Expr cond, Expr then, Expr els)` is bound by
// seventeen arms and `case Ternary(cond, els, then)` compiles, type-checks, and inverts every
// conditional in the program. `Binary(i32 op, Expr left, Expr right)` is twenty-six arms and turns
// `a - b` into `b - a`. `Index(Expr arr, Expr index)` is seventeen and makes `a[i]` into `i[a]`.
// `StructDecl` has seven fields and fifty-four arms; `Func` has seven and forty-three, including
// `bool exported` and `bool isAsync`, which are adjacent, identical and mean opposite things.
//
// None of that is hypothetical arithmetic: those are the arms in the tree today, and every one of
// them is a position a reader has to count.

import { Vec } from "core";

/**
 * A token index — `i32` in the original, at every one of the tree's leaves.
 *
 * `Ident(i32 tok)`, `Binary(i32 op, …)`, `Jsx(i32 tagTok, i32 closeTok, …)`: three different
 * meanings of `i32` in one enum, and `Jsx`'s two are the same meaning twice. A distinct type costs
 * nothing at runtime — `../../DECISIONS.md` has `export Slice<u8> Bytes;` as the form — and it makes
 * `Binary(op, left, right)` un-swappable with `Jsx(tagTok, closeTok, …)` even before anything below
 * happens.
 *
 * It does **not** fix the case this file is about: `Jsx`'s two `Tok`s are still two `Tok`s. A name
 * is the only thing that separates two fields of one type, which is the whole argument.
 */
export Tok i32;

/** Where a node came from. Unchanged; it is already a struct, so it is already named. */
export struct Span { i32 line; i32 col; }

export struct Expr { ExprKind kind; Span at; }

/**
 * The expressions.
 *
 * Written with the same field names the original has, because the names are not the problem — they
 * exist and are good. What is missing is any way to *use* them.
 */
export enum ExprKind {
  IntLit(Tok tok),
  FloatLit(Tok tok),
  StrLit(Tok tok),
  Ident(Tok tok),

  Unary(Tok op, Expr operand),
  Binary(Tok op, Expr left, Expr right),
  Ternary(Expr cond, Expr then, Expr els),
  Index(Expr arr, Expr index),
  Cast(Tok op, Expr operand, Ty target),
  Call(Expr callee, Vec<Expr> args, Vec<Ty> typeArgs),

  Jsx(Tok tagTok, Tok closeTok, Vec<JsxAttr> attrs, Vec<Expr> kids),
  JsxText(i32 startOff, i32 endOff),

  MatchExpr(Expr subject, Vec<Arm> arms)
}

// ## 1. The brace pattern is half a fix, and it is the half that exists
//
// `../../GRAMMAR.ebnf` proposes `Ok { v }:` and `Err { .. }:` — a payload bound by field name. It is
// one of the constructs with no user anywhere, and its production is one of the eight whose removal
// changes nothing in the tree.
//
// `src/walk.wac` is its first consumer, and the case is the 291 arms above. Two things fall out that
// the proposal does not say.
//
// **It borrows the struct-literal syntax and nobody wrote down that it is the same idea.** `Point {
// x: 3, y: 4 }` is construction by name and `Ternary { cond, then }` is destructuring by name; the
// braces are the same braces and the field names are the same field names. Presenting the second as
// a new pattern form, rather than as *the enum payload finally reaching the struct rule*, is what
// makes it look like a convenience.
//
// **`..` is doing a second job and should be argued separately.** `Err { .. }` means *ignore the
// rest*, which the positional form spells `Err(_)` — one wildcard for the whole payload. But
// `[§wac-arm-partial]` already lets an arm ignore one field at a time positionally, so `{ .. }`
// duplicates something rather than adding it. What is genuinely new is *naming a subset*:
// `Func { nameTok, body, .. }` binds two of seven, and that is the thing fifty-four `StructDecl`
// arms want.
//
// ## 2. And the other half is construction, which nothing proposes
//
// A brace pattern fixes *reading* a payload. Writing one is still positional, and the write side is
// where the worst case lives:
//
//     Decl(DeclKind.Func(t, ty, ps, body, true, tps, false), at)
//
// Two bare booleans, four fields apart, meaning `exported` and `isAsync`. Swap them and the compiler
// is silent, every diagnostic still points at the right line, and an unexported function becomes an
// exported synchronous one. The brace pattern does nothing about this line, and it is the same
// fields the pattern is for.
//
// **The syntax for it already exists twice over.** `Point { x: 3, y: 4 }` is a struct literal, and
// `i32[n](fill: -1)` is a named argument in a call position — with the spec's own reason:
// *"Named argument syntax cannot collide, since a call rejects it outright."* So the language has
// `name: value` in a construction, has it order-independent, has it optional for a nullable field,
// and an enum variant gets none of it. `Func { nameTok: t, exported: true, isAsync: false, … }`
// needs no new notation at all — it needs the rule that already applies to `Point` to apply to a
// payload.
//
// That is the promotion this file exists to make: **not *add a brace pattern*, but *an enum's
// payload is a struct and is not treated as one*.** The pattern is one direction of that and is
// worth having; on its own it fixes 291 arms and leaves every construction site as it was.
//
// ## 3. What could not be written
//
// **A nullable payload field cannot be omitted.** `[§wacc-struct-nullable-optional]` lets a struct
// literal leave one out. `ArrNew(Ty elem, Expr? size, Expr? fill, Vec<Expr> elements)` has two
// nullables and every construction passes `null` for at least one of them, positionally. If a
// payload were a struct this would follow from the rule that already exists, and it is the clearest
// evidence that the two things really are one thing.
//
// **A payload field cannot be `const`.** A struct field can. This is `issues/lang/open/0315a`'s
// territory rather than this file's, and it is another row in the same table of *what a struct has
// and a payload does not*.
//
// **The tree is still a tree of indices and this rewrite does not change that.** `Tok` is a name for
// an `i32`; `Expr` holds an `ExprKind` which holds `Expr`s. Whether that should be an arena, a
// handle type, or references is a question about wasm GC and about `../../DECISIONS.md`'s rule that
// references are comparable but not hashable — not about payloads, and much larger than this.
